      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Introduction",
      "pageStart": 10,
      "pageEnd": 20,
      "target": "../sections/sec-003-introduction.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Health products pass through multiple supply chain \npartners and processes as they move from manufacturers to \nend-users. For transactions to be seamless, all stakeholders \nmust consistently identify health products using \nstandardized information.\nFor example, if a country’s central warehouse receives a \nshipment of vaccines with identifiers that do not match its \nstandardized data, manual reconciliation will be required. \nThis can lead to delays, errors, and distribution disruptions, \nultimately impacting health outcomes.\nUniform and standardized product information builds trust \namong suppl",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Introduction explaining product master data versus transactional data and why consistent product identification matters across supply chain partners, then situating the handbook within the Digital implementation investment guide (DIIG), WHO SMART Guidelines (layers L1 to L5) and the digital health supply chain architecture's product registry; it also outlines the chapters, how the handbook was developed, target users per chapter, and when to use it.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
